  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected tonight as another \ndisturbance is expected to move through the area. Being on the cool \nside of this system, no severe weather is expected. Precipitation \ncomes to an end from west to east after midnight and be confined to \ncentral Kansas Thursday morning. The current NBM model run depicts a \nhalf of an inch to three quarters of an inch of precipitation by \ntomorrow afternoon. Mostly cloudy skies are expected through \ntomorrow morning with clearing in the afternoon. The reminder of the \nforecast looks dry as upper level ridging builds across the western \nUnited States with high pressure being the dominate feature at the \nsurface. Winds will be from the north through Friday, shifting to \nthe south this weekend into the beginning of next week. As for \ntemperatures, expect highs to only reach into the 50s this afternoon \ndue to the copious amounts of cloudiness and drizzle/rain. Highs \ntomorrow look to range from the upper 50s across our northern zones \nto upper 60s along the KS/OK border. If clouds remain longer than \nexpected, highs may be lower than expected. Friday looks to be the \ncoldest day of the forecast with highs in the 50s. We then rebound \ninto the 60s Saturday with 70s returning Sunday into the first part \nof next week. Lows will dip into the mid 40s to upper 50s tonight \nwith upper 30s tomorrow night. Lows around 30 degrees to low 30s are \nthen expected Saturday morning. A freeze watch may be needed across \nfar western Kansas in the future to account for freezing \ntemperatures. Lows then rebound into the 40s by the beginning of \nnext week.",
